// F206: `push`, `insert` and `set` had one `typecheck.c` arm between them, and the sharing showed
// in the messages. `expected_argc` came from the *name* before argument one had been looked at, so
// `insert(ms, 2)` on a MutableSet was told it "takes exactly 3 argument(s)" -- an arity no
// MutableSet `insert` has, there being no such `insert` -- and every rejected first argument got
// the same cross-reference, "(or, for set/insert/remove, a map or set)", whether or not the name it
// had been given takes one: a MutableString was told about maps and sets instead of about `append`.
//
// Arity is a property of the (name, argument-one) pair, not of the name. Each line below is a
// shape that used to get the wrong answer; the .diag pins which message each type now gets, since
// the point of the split is *that they differ by type*, which a .err fixture cannot say.
fn main() {
    val m = Map<string, int> { "a": 1 }
    val s = Set<int> { 1 }
    val ms = mutable(s)
    val mm = mutable(m)
    val b = mutable([1])

    // `push` takes the two array shapes and nothing else; one sentence, the same whatever it
    // rejected, which is what lets it become an `#[when_unmatched]` line when `push` is converted.
    val p1 = push(m, 1)
    val p2 = push(s, 2)

    // `insert` is positional only -- F199's decision, which the shared arm went on contradicting.
    val i1 = insert(s, 2)
    insert(ms, 2)
    insert(mm, "b", 2)

    // `set`'s two refusals are keyed on the type it rejected, and that is what keeps it arm-handled.
    val s1 = set(s, 2)
    set(ms, 2)
    set(b, 0, 9)

    print(len(p1) + len(p2) + len(i1) + len(s1))
}
